the first thing we want to do is we want  to analyze the horizontal asymptote and  the way you do that is you look at the  highest degree term in the numerator and  the highest degree term in the  denominator and you analyze their powers  their degrees so in this case the  highest degree term here is this one the  highest degree term here is this one but  you can see that the denominators degree  is higher than the numerators highest  power term right and so what happens as  X gets larger and larger meaning as you  go to the right or the left okay on this  graph as X gets larger and larger like a  positive number or a large larger  negative number this is growing faster  meaning the deniers going much faster  than the numerator and what happens is  the fresh and overall gets smaller  smaller smaller approaches zero so for  these ones you're gonna get a horizontal  asymptote y equals zero it's gonna be  right on the x axis okay so it's gonna  be approaching that line now if you are  not sure not confident about it just go  ahead and pick a large value like say  you pick a thousand a thousand squared  is a million minus nine see the nine  doesn't make much of an impact it's  still like a lot of million and then  this was a would we say it was a  thousand thousand minus three the three  doesn't make much of a difference but we  have a thousand over a million which is  one one thousandth which is point zero  zero one so you can see it's getting  very small so you could approach it that  way by picking out a number a larger  number and seeing what happens but again  the degree here in the dimer is greater  than the numerator so we're getting y  equal to zero for a horizontal asymptote  now on this one you can see that the  highest degree term in the numerator and  the highest degree term in the  denominator do the same they don't have  to be squared they can be cubed or  fourth degrees and so forth but if  they're the same that means that this  term is growing at the same rate as this  term if these terms are growing much  faster than these two terms okay here so  if we cover those up but the graphs  going to behave like this as X gets very  large and because you're growing at the  same rate what we do is we get the ratio  of the coefficient so in this case it's  going to be one over to our horizontal  asymptote is going to be y equals  one-half and then the third scenario is  where the degree in the numerator is  greater than the degree in the  denominator here's where we get like a  slant or an oblique or a diagonal  asymptote and the way you would do that  is you would take this numerator and you  would divide it by the denominator you  could just do the long division and  you're going to get the equation of a  slant asymptotes whether this one I'll  just show you X is going into x squared  plus zero  plus one let's see x times X is x  squared if we subtract we're getting  let's see just one left over this is a  lower degree than this this would be  plus one over X but as X gets really  large this term actually goes to zero so  it's really in this case just y equals x  so we have a slant asymptote that looks  like this on a 45 degree angle or a  slope of 1 okay so that's the slant  asymptote so this is discussing the  horizontal and the slant asymptotes now  the vertical asymptotes what you want to  do you want to factor the numerator and  denominator as much as you can and the  reason is is because I'll give you an  example say for example you factor your  ok rational function let's say you get  something like this X minus 2 over X  plus 2 over X minus 2 X plus 3 now when  you look at the denominator that's how  you analyze the vertical asymptotes you  can't divide by zero so here you can see  that X can't be negative 3 ok X cannot  be negative 3 and X cannot be 2 and you  might jump to the conclusion a lot of  students do that we have a vertical  asymptote at 2 and a vertical asymptote  at negative 3 but because this term in  the denominator is cancelling with this  term in the numerator we're actually not  going to get a vertical asymptote there  we're going to get what's called a  removable discontinuity or a hole in  there raph you can check out the video I  did on that if you want to learn more  about the removal discontinuities but  for this video we're just going to talk  about the vertical asymptotes now and so  you're going to have a vertical  asymptote at x equals negative 3 so  that's going to be 1 2 3  and there's your vertical asymptote like  so so the key is you want to factor the  numerator and denominator if any factors  cancel out you don't get a vertical  asymptote at those values you just get a  hole so we're going to just cancel those  out we're gonna look at the other  denominator X cannot be negative 3 and  that's where your vertical asymptote  occurs so I hope this little video  helped you understand how to find  horizontal and vertical asymptotes when  you're graphing rational functions check  out some of the other videos I did  talking about graphing rational  functions to go a little bit deeper and  understand this even better  subscribe to the channel check out some  more Matthew do videos on Mario's math  tutor and YouTube channel and I look  forward to seeing the future videos I'll  talk to you soon
 

